{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact head 075fa9a36a27cce06d57a2726b23a19a61a02be3.\\n\\nArchitecture: The draft keeps note interpretation above the kernel, which is the right Workroom application-profile boundary, and exact note paths avoid a repository-wide mutex. But it changes the governed Workroom contract while claiming no fold change. Artifact has SatisfierNone, so ratify(artifact) is ineffective; review approval is ratified only by its originating requester, and merge is restricted to the approved implementer. Use a separate ratifiable proposal resting on the note artifact, or explicitly design the vocabulary/fold change and update docs/reference/architecture.md in that implementation. The hook also cannot copy implementing commit Rests-On trailers: assigned commits rest on the request while their reporting artifacts rest on the promise. Replacement needs an explicit merge-sealed map because same-path publication does not retire a predecessor, candidate N to S provenance is not preserved by current merge succession, and branch pushes otherwise accumulate live candidate artifacts.\\n\\nSecurity: Pre-push occurs before remote acceptance, so it can record false publication on a rejected push, while the default non-blocking failure creates an invisible omission that an identical retry may never repair. It must not perform irreversible supersession from branch-controlled .gitseq or front matter. Clone-wide gitseq.actor can misattribute concurrent linked worktrees; require explicit process or worktree-scoped identity and fail closed. Keep the existing loopback-only resident boundary. Define bounded NUL-safe handling for refs, paths, renames, deletions, tags, force pushes, trailers, YAML, act count, partial batch recovery, and ineffective acts. The installer must preserve existing hooks, include a shebang and executable mode, handle shared core.hooksPath safely, and avoid ambient executable resolution. Pushing main must detect and skip the exact path and commit artifacts already produced by gs merge.\\n\\nSimplification: Split this into independently reviewable work: decision adoption, publication/reconciliation, and replacement/merge semantics. The smallest supported decision lifecycle is ordinary Git content plus exact-head artifact, independent approval, requester ratification, merge, and a separate ratified proposal for governance authority; implementation can cite both the proposal and the live post-merge artifact. Prefer an explicit post-success publish command or push wrapper with a durable outbox and reconciliation over a best-effort pre-push attestation. Keep merge-message rendering manual and non-authoritative in the first cut. This removes the impossible artifact-ratification rule and defers automatic replacement until merge can seal and preserve it.\\n\\nChecks: make docs passed at the exact checkout; git diff --check passed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2c84b1da4371dacfe701afb185853d9fa0d595f6","head":"075fa9a36a27cce06d57a2726b23a19a61a02be3","verdict":"changes-requested"}}